<clickhouse>
    <!--
    Bound the caches to the container.

    ClickHouse sizes these for a dedicated machine, and nothing here had ever
    overridden them. The defaults are:

        mark_cache_size          5 GiB
        uncompressed_cache_size  8 GiB

    against a pod whose memory limit is 4Gi. The mark cache alone is allowed
    to grow larger than the entire container, and max_server_memory_usage is
    derived from the cgroup at 90 percent, so the server will happily fill
    3.6 GiB with cache it was never told it could not have.

    What that looks like when it goes wrong is not an out of memory kill. The
    OvercommitTracker picks a query to cancel once resident memory crosses the
    ceiling, and the query it picks is whichever happens to be running, not
    whichever caused the pressure. On the reporting cluster that was a plain
    count over ten thousand rows:

        Code: 241. DB::Exception: (total) memory limit exceeded: would use
        2.60 GiB, current RSS: 3.61 GiB, maximum: 3.60 GiB. OvercommitTracker
        decision: Query was selected to stop by OvercommitTracker
        (query: SELECT count() FROM mirror.orders_current)

    Note the shape of it: tracked memory 2.60 GiB, resident 3.61 GiB. The
    gigabyte between them is cache and allocator, not query working set, and
    the count was the victim rather than the cause. Reading that error as "the
    count is expensive" leads to rewriting a query that was never the problem.

    512 MiB of marks is ample for four mirror tables holding a few hundred
    thousand rows, and the uncompressed cache is off because queries do not
    use it unless use_uncompressed_cache is set, which nothing here sets.
    -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
